{"slug": "generator-excitation-system-avr", "targets": ["excitation system"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">Excitation System Components and Power Sources Quick Reference</h2><p><strong>An excitation system</strong> supplies and controls the DC field current of a synchronous generator. Since induced voltage follows E = 4.44 f N &Phi;, the excitation system raises or lowers field current to hold terminal voltage steady as load changes.</p><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Main Building Blocks</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Component</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Function</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Exciter</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Produces the DC power fed to the rotor field winding</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">AVR</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Compares terminal voltage with the set point and adjusts exciter output</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Rotating rectifier</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Diodes on the shaft that convert exciter AC to DC in brushless designs</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Limiters</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Keep field current within over excitation and under excitation limits</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">PSS</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Adds damping to rotor oscillations on grid connected machines</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Protection</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Field failure, over voltage and over excitation trips</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Where the Field Power Comes From</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Type</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Power source</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Brushes</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">DC exciter</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">DC generator on the main shaft</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Yes, commutator and slip rings</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Brushless AC exciter</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">AC exciter with rotating diodes</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">No</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Static excitation</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Generator terminals through transformer and SCR bridge</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Yes, slip rings</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">PMG supported</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Permanent magnet generator on the shaft feeding the AVR</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">No</td></tr></table></div><p>Static systems respond fastest because no rotating exciter time constant is involved, while PMG supported designs keep AVR power available during faults.</p>"}